/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 27

 1189                 /       ADDITIONAL SYSTEM-DEPENDENT P?S/8 COMPONENTS.
 1190   
 1191                 /       UNLIKE OS/8, P?S/8 HAS ADDITIONAL COMPONENTS SPECIFIC TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE
 1192                 /       TYPE.  WHEN A SYSTEM IS CREATED, ALL DEVICE-SPECIFIC CODE OVERLAYS AN
 1193                 /       OTHERWISE GENERIC OPERATING SYSTEM AND RELATED COMPONENTS.
 1194   
 1195                 /       REWIND AND UNLOAD ROUTINE.
 1196   
 1197                 /       THE P?S8 KEYBOARD MONITOR SUPPORTS TWO COMMANDS DESIGNATED FOR TAPE-BASED OR
 1198                 /       OTHER SYSTEMS WHERE THE SYSTEM DEVICE CAN BE REWOUND AND/OR UNLOADED; THE
 1199                 /       TEMPLATE FOR THESE COMMANDS IS THE IMPLEMENTATION FOR THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE
 1200                 /       CONTROLLER.  ON THIS CONFIGURATION, COMMANDS CAN BE TYPED THAT ALLOW OVERLAP
 1201                 /       OF TAPE MOTION WITH KEYBOARD MONITOR COMMANDS [CONTROL-R TO REWIND THE TAPE]
 1202                 /       AND UNLOAD THE THE SYSTEM DEVICE ENTIRELY [WHICH REQUIRES WAITING FOR THE
 1203                 /       OPERATION TO COMPLETE].
 1204   
 1205                 /       WHERE POSSIBLE, ATTEMPT TO MIMIC [OR IMPROVE] THIS BEHAVIOR FOR EACH
 1206                 /       CONFIGURATION.  THE TD8E CONTROLLER CANNOT ALLOW OVERLAP WITH TAPE REWIND
 1207                 /       BECAUSE THERE IS NO AUTOMATIC END ZONE DETECTION; AS SUCH, THE SPECIFIC
 1208                 /       IMPLEMENTATION DIFFERS IN SOME WAYS.  BECAUSE THERE ARE NO BRAKE-RELATED
 1209                 /       ISSUES, THE UNLOAD OPERATION CAN BE INITIATED AND WILL NOT WAIT FOR COMPLETION
 1210                 /       [HOWEVER, THE USER HAS TO STOP THE TAPE FROM INFINITE FLAPPING].
 1211   
 1212           0000          FIELD   0                       /FIELD WHERE THIS CODE IS WRITTEN OUT FROM
 1213                                                         /WHEN GENERATING A COMPLETE OPERATING SYSTEM.
 1214   
 1215          *1546          *REWIND-1                       /THESE ROUTINES ARE NOT RELOCATED.
 1216   
 1217   001546  3000  L3000,  3000                            /CONSTANT 3000.
 1218   
 1219                 /       THE ROUTINES ARE ENTERED HERE.  OTHER THAN COMMON OPERATIONS, EACH COMMAND
 1220                 /       WILL PERFORM ADDITIONAL PROGRAMMING TO COMPLETE THE SPECIFIC TASK.
 1221   
 1222   001547  4356  REWIND, JMS     TAPMOVE                 /THE AC CONTAINS 0000, START A REWIND
 1223                                                         /ON THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.
 1224   001550  1100          TAD     TEMP1                   /GET LATEST CHARACTER.
 1225   001551  1064          TAD     L7756/[-"R!300]         /COMPARE TO <^R>.
 1226   001552  7650          SNA CLA                         /SKIP IF OTHER [<^T>].
 1227   001553  5364          JMP     ENDWAIT                 /WAIT FOR END ZONE THERE.
 1228   
 1229                 /       WE WANT TO UNLOAD THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT; SELECT THE OTHER DRIVE WHILE
 1230                 /       INITIATING MOTION AS WELL [AND THEN TURN MOTION OFF ON THE OTHER UNIT].  THE
 1231                 /       DESELECTION PROCESS DONE THIS WAY WILL CAUSE THE ORIGINAL UNIT TO FALL OFF THE
 1232                 /       TAKEUP REEL.
 1233   
 1234   001554  7201          NL0001                          /SET UNIT REVERSAL BIT.
 1235   001555  5373          JMP     DESELECT                /CONTINUE THERE.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 28

 1237                 /       TAPE MOVE IN REVERSE ROUTINE.  CALL WITH AC=0000 TO REWIND THE CURRENT SYSTEM
 1238                 /       DEVICE UNIT; CALL WITH AC=0001 TO REWIND THE OPPOSITE SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.
 1239   
 1240   001556  0000  TAPMOVE,.-.                             /REWIND THE TAPE ROUTINE.
 1241   001557  1000          TAD     UNIT                    /ADD ON THE CURRENT LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT.
 1242   001560  7112          CLL RTR                         /MOVE DESIRED UNIT TO AC[0].
 1243   001561  1346          TAD     L3000/(3000)            /ADD ON REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION BITS.
 1244   001562  6774          SDLC                            /LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER.
 1245   001563  5756          JMP I   TAPMOVE                 /RETURN TO CALLER.
 1246   
 1247                 /       COMES HERE TO WAIT FOR THE END ZONE ON THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.
 1248   
 1249   001564  6771  ENDWAIT,SDSS                            /SINGLE-LINE FLAG UP?
 1250   001565  5364          JMP     .-1                     /NO, WAIT FOR IT
 1251   001566  6776          SDRC                            /YES, GET THE COMMAND REGISTER CONTENTS.
 1252   001567  0056          AND     L77/[77]                /JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
 1253   001570  1064          TAD     L7756/[-22]             /COMPARE TO END ZONE VALUE.
 1254   001571  7640          SZA CLA                         /SKIP IF IN END ZONE NOW.
 1255   001572  5364          JMP     ENDWAIT                 /NOT YET, KEEP WAITING.
 1256   
 1257                 /       WE'RE IN THE END ZONE HAVING REWOUND THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.  FALL
 1258                 /       THROUGH TO THE NEXT ROUTINE WHICH WILL REDUNDANTLY SELECT THE SAME SYSTEM
 1259                 /       DEVICE UNIT, THEN CLEAR THE MOTION BIT.
 1260   
 1261           1573          DESELEC=.                       /DESELECT THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT HERE.
 1262   
 1263                 /       IF THE AC IS SET TO 0001, THE OTHER SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT WILL BE SELECTED WITH
 1264                 /       REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION SET.  THIS WILL CAUSE THE CURRENT DEVICE UNIT
 1265                 /       [WHICH WAS SELECTED FOR REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION SET] TO LOSE CONTROL; THE
 1266                 /       TAPE WILL COMPLETELY UNLOAD AND NOT STOP TAPE FLAPPING UNTIL THE OPERATOR
 1267                 /       STOPS THE TAPE MANUALLY.  IF THE AC IS SET TO 0000 THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE
 1268                 /       UNIT WILL BE REDUNDANTLY SELECTED FOR REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION [WHICH IS
 1269                 /       HARMLESS].
 1270   
 1271   001573  4356          JMS     TAPMOVE                 /SELECT EITHER THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE
 1272                                                         /LOGICAL UNIT OR THE OTHER SYSTEM DEVICE
 1273                                                         /LOGICAL UNIT [DEPENDING ON HOW WE GOT HERE].
 1274   001574  6776          SDRC                            /GET THE CURRENT COMMAND REGISTER.
 1275   001575  1077          TAD     L7000/[-1000]           /REMOVE THE MOTION BIT.
 1276                 /       TAD     L7000/(7000)            /REMOVE THE MOTION BIT. **** VERSION 8Z ****
 1277   001576  6774          SDLC                            /LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER TO
 1278                                                         /STOP THE CURRENT DECTAPE UNIT.
 1279   001577  5216          JMP     CHLOOP                  /KEEP GOING THERE.
 1280   
 1281                 /L7000, 7000                            /CONSTANT 7000 **** VERSION 8Z ****
 1282   
 1283                 /       ZBLOCK  .!177-.                 /EMPTY SPACE [IF ANY]. **** VERSION 8Z ****
 1284   
 1285                 /       JMP I   PCHLOOP/[CHLOOP]        /CONTINUE THERE. **** VERSION 8Z ****


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 29

 1287                 /       DEVICE LOGICAL SIZE.
 1288   
 1289                 /       THE SYSTEM GENERATION ROUTINES NEED TO KNOW THE OVERALL SIZE OF ANY ONE DEVICE
 1290                 /       ADDRESSED BY THE SYSTEM HANDLER; CERTAIN ENTRIES WILL BE MADE IN VARIOUS
 1291                 /       STRUCTURES BASED ON THIS VALUE.  [AS A PRACTICAL MATTER, THIS SIZE MUST BE
 1292                 /       GREATER THAN 1000 BLOCKS TO BE OF MUCH USE.]
 1293   
 1294                 /       ON VERY LARGE DEVICES, THIS COULD BE AS MUCH AS 10000 BLOCKS [WHICH SHOULD BE
 1295                 /       EXPRESSED AS 0000]; THIS IS THE UPPER LIMIT OF ADDRESSABILITY FOR ANY P?S/8
 1296                 /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER [BUT MAY NOT BE THE UPPER LIMIT OF THE ACTUAL DEVICE].
 1297   
 1298                 /       THE P?S/8 SHELL IS CAPABLE OF ADDRESSING MORE STORAGE BLOCKS THAN THE BASIC
 1299                 /       P?S/8 SYSTEM THROUGH THE USE OF NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS.  FOR EXAMPLE, ON
 1300                 /       AN RK8E/RK05 SYSTEM CONFIGURED FOR LOGICAL DRIVES 0 THROUGH 3 [WHERE EACH
 1301                 /       LOGICAL UNIT IS AN ENTIRE RK05 PACK], THE MAXIMUM POSSIBLE SYSTEM BLOCK
 1302                 /       AVAILABLE TO THE BASIC SYSTEM IS 07777 AS DEFINED ABOVE.  HOWEVER, THE MAXIMUM
 1303                 /       BLOCK AVAILABLE TO THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT WOULD BE 31277, WHERE BLOCKS
 1304                 /       10000-31277 ARE ONLY ACCESSIBLE THROUGH NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS AS USED IN THE
 1305                 /       SHELL ENVIRONMENT [AS WELL AS THE BASIC SYSTEM PROGRAM "BLKCPY" WHEN IT IS
 1306                 /       UPGRADED TO THE NEW SPECIFICATION FOR NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS IN THE VERSION 9A
 1307                 /       RELEASE].  AS SUCH, BLOCKS 00000-07777 REPRESENT THE MAXIMUM VALUE FOR THE
 1308                 /       BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM THAT CAN BE CONFIGURED; SMALLER VALUES OF MAXIMUM BLOCK MAY
 1309                 /       BE USED IN PRACTICAL CONFIGURATIONS WHERE THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM IS NOT
 1310                 /       REQUIRED TO SUPPORT THAT MANY STORAGE BLOCKS.  THE SYSTEM MAY BE CONFIGURED
 1311                 /       SUCH THAT MORE OF THE AVAILABLE STORAGE IS ALLOCATED TO THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT
 1312                 /       AS REQUIRED.  [NOTE: IT IS POSSIBLE TO CONFIGURE A BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM TO ONLY
 1313                 /       ADDRESS BLOCKS BLOCKS 00000-00377 OR PERHAPS SOMEWHAT LESS, WITH THE REST OF
 1314                 /       THE DEVICE STORAGE CONFIGURED FOR USE IN THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT ONLY.]
 1315   
 1316          *6760          *DEVSIZE                        /OVER SYSTEM GENERATION ROUTINES.
 1317   
 1318   006760  2702          TDSIZE                          /DEFINE OUR DEVICE SIZE.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 30

 1320                 /       P?S/8 BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER AND BOOTSTRAP FOR THE TD8E WITH MR8E-C ROM.
 1321   
 1322           0002          FIELD   SLPGEN&70%10            /GENERATION FIELD FOR SLURP LOADER.
 1323   
 1324          *4400          *SLPGEN&7600                    /GENERATION ADDRESS FOR SLURP LOADER.
 1325   
 1326                 SLPCORE,NOPUNCH                         /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1327   
 1328           0000          FIELD   0                       /WHERE IT LOADS LATER.
 1329   
 1330          *4400          *SLPCORE                        /RESET THE ORIGIN BACK IN CASE FIELD CHANGES IT.
 1331   
 1332                         ENPUNCH                         /UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1333   
 1334          *7600          RELOC   SBOOT                   /WHERE THE SLURP LOADER AND BOOTSTRAP EXECUTES.
 1335   
 1336                 /       DEVICE DEPENDENT BOOTSTRAP STARTING AT 07600.  ALSO USED WITH THE P?S/8
 1337                 /       VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE "BIN" LOADER [INSTEAD OF THE SLURP LOADER].
 1338   
 1339                 /       P?S/8 BOOTSTRAP CONVENTIONS ARE AS FOLLOWS:
 1340   
 1341                 /       1) AC CONTENTS UNIMPORTANT.
 1342   
 1343                 /       2) LINK CONTENTS UNIMPORTANT.
 1344   
 1345                 /       3) IN GENERAL, THE INTERRUPT SYSTEM SHOULD BE TURNED OFF; HOWEVER, BECAUSE
 1346                 /          INTERRUPTS ARE PARTICULARLY A PROBLEM FOR TD8E-BASED SYSTEMS, THIS
 1347                 /          BOOTSTRAP FORCES INTERRUPTS OFF AS THERE IS SUFFICIENT CODE SPACE FOR THE
 1348                 /          "IOF" INSTRUCTION.  [NOTE: IF THE SYSTEM DEVICE OPERATES BY DMA OR PROGRAM
 1349                 /          TRANSFER WITHOUT CRITICAL TIMING, THIS WILL NOT BE A PROBLEM.  THE CODING
 1350                 /          OF THE P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR AND (EMBEDDED EDITOR) IS SUCH THAT INTERRUPTS CAUSE AN ADDITIONAL SYSTEM RESTART WITH
 1351                 /          INTERRUPTS TURNED OFF.]
 1352   
 1353                 /       4) THE DATA FIELD MUST BE 0.
 1354   
 1355                 /       5) ALL WORDS OF THE BOOTSTRAP MUST BE INTACT.  AS LONG AS ONLY WELL-BEHAVED
 1356                 /          PROGRAMS ARE LOADED, THIS SHOULD NEVER BE A PROBLEM.
 1357   
 1358                 SBOOT,                                  /BOOTSTRAP RELOAD STARTS HERE.
 1359   007600* 6002          IOF                             /TURN INTERRUPTS OFF.
 1360   007601* 7670  B7670,  SNA SZL CLA/(-110)              /CONSTANT 7670; CLEARS THE AC AND MIGHT SKIP.
 1361   007602* 7470  BROMBOO,ROMBOOT/(SNA SZL)               /ENTRY POINT INTO THE ROM TO REBOOT; CANNOT
 1362                                                         /SKIP BECAUSE THE AC IS NOW CLEAR.
 1363   007603* 6272          CIF     ROMFLD                  /GOING TO ROM FIELD.
 1364   007604* 5602          JMP I   BROMBOOT/(ROMBOOT)      /GO TO BOOTUP ROUTINE.
 1365   
 1366           7605          SLBTEND=.                       /ACTUAL END OF BOOTSTRAP-REQUIRED CODE.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 31

 1368                 /       NOTE: AS OF VERSION 8Z, THERE IS AN SPECIFIC UPPER LIMIT ON THE SIZE OF THE
 1369                 /       CODE NEEDED TO REBOOT P?S/8 WHEN REMOVED FROM THE SLURP LOADER [TO BE USED
 1370                 /       WITH THE P?S/8 VERSIONS OF THE BIN AND RIM LOADERS].  VARIOUS EARLIER RELEASES
 1371                 /       MAY HAVE MADE DIFFERENT DEMANDS ON THIS CODE LENGTH [AND VERSION 9A WILL
 1372                 /       CHANGE THIS UPPER LIMIT TO ONE WORD LESS].  IT IS NOT EXPECTED THIS NEW
 1373                 /       REQUIREMENT WILL AFFECT TOO MANY SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS; EACH WILL HAVE TO BE
 1374                 /       RECODED AS REQUIRED.  HOWEVER, THIS CONFIGURATION USES ONE OF THE SMALLEST
 1375                 /       BOOTUP SECTIONS; AS SUCH, NO CHANGES WILL BE NEEDED FOR THE P?S/8 TD8E WITH
 1376                 /       MR8E-C ROM VARIANT.  [NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM, ONCE REASSEMBLED FOR USE
 1377                 /       WITH VERSION 9A, IS ONE WORD SHORTER THAN THE NEW LIMITATION.  EACH SYSTEM
 1378                 /       DEVICE HANDLER WILL BE EVALUATED REGARDING THIS POINT.]
 1379   
 1380                 /       ALL BOOTSTRAP CODE MUST BE WITHIN 07600-07642 IN P?S/8 VERSION 8Z.  [NOTE:
 1381                 /       IN VERSION 9A, THIS WILL BE FURTHER RESTRICTED TO 07600-07641.  THIS WILL
 1382                 /       FACILITATE IMPROVING THE P?S/8 VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE "BIN" LOADER BY
 1383                 /       ALLOWING ONE ADDITIONAL WORD TO BE AVAILABLE.  THIS WILL BE USED TO IMPROVE
 1384                 /       THE LOADER; WHEN COMPLETED, IT WILL BE IMMUNE TO INTERNAL DESTRUCTION BY THE
 1385                 /       EXECUTION OF ALL PROGRAMS FOR THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK DEVICES.  BY CONTRAST,
 1386                 /       NO OTHER VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE BIN LOADER IS ROBUST ENOUGH TO PREVENT
 1387                 /       DESTRUCTION BY THE EXECUTION OF PROGRAMS FOR AT LEAST ONE OF THE PREVAILING
 1388                 /       DEVICES [DF32/RF08, TC58 MAGNETIC TAPE, TC01/TC08 DECTAPE].
 1389   
 1390                 /       THE SLURP LOADER AND BOOTSTRAP ARE WRITTEN OUT TO A SYSTEM-DESIGNATED BLOCK.
 1391                 /       THE P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM WILL USE THE ENTIRE BLOCK CONTENTS WHEN LOADING TFS
 1392                 /       BINARY FILES; ONLY THE BOOTSTRAP WILL BE USED IN CONJUNCTION WITH THE P?S/8
 1393                 /       VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE "BIN" AND "RIM" LOADERS.  THIS WILL ALLOW RELOAD OF
 1394                 /       P?S/8 AS LONG AS THE LOCATIONS FROM 07600-ONWARD ARE NOT DESTROYED BY THE
 1395                 /       PROGRAMS LOADED BY BIN [OR RIM].
 1396   
 1397                 /       ENSURE THIS SIZE CONVENTION IS MET.
 1398   
 1399                         IFNZRO  SBOOT+BBLEN-.&4000      <ERROR  .>
 1401   
 1402                 /       DETAILED DESCRIPTION OF SLURP FORMAT P?S/8 BINARY FILES.
 1403   
 1404                 /       EVERY BLOCK OF A SLURP FILE IS INDEPENDENT OF ALL OTHER BLOCKS OTHER THAN ANY
 1405                 /       CONSIDERATION OF CURRENT LOADING ORIGIN AND CURRENT LOADING FIELD INHERITED
 1406                 /       FROM THE PREVIOUS BLOCK PROCESSED.  TO ENSURE PROPER LOADING, EVERY SLURP FILE
 1407                 /       CONTAINS INITIAL FIELD 0 AND ORIGIN 0200 SETTINGS; THIS AVOIDS DEPENDENCE ON
 1408                 /       ORDER OF LOADING, EVEN IN THE CASE OF MULTIPLE OUTPUT FILES CREATED BY A
 1409                 /       SINGLE ASSEMBLY [OR BINARY PAPER-TAPE CONVERSION].  SINCE THIS FIXED SEQUENCE
 1410                 /       IS PART OF THE OPENING WORDS OF A BINARY FILE, CONFORMING PROGRAMS CAN
 1411                 /       DETERMINE THE FILE IS BINARY.  [NOTE: P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS SUCH AS "FOCAL"
 1412                 /       USE BOTH BINARY AND TEXT FILES; BY USING THIS FIXED SEQUENCE AS PART OF A
 1413                 /       SIGNATURE CHECK, THE PROGRAM IS ABLE TO DIFFERENTIATE AND PROCESS THE FILES
 1414                 /       PASSED IN A MIXED SEQUENCE; FOCAL REQUIRES ALL BINARY FILES TO BE PASSED PRIOR
 1415                 /       TO ALL TEXT FILES, ETC.]
 1416   
 1417                 /       WHILE TFS FILES ARE RESTRICTED TO AT MOST 16 BLOCKS OF DATA, THE SLURP FORMAT
 1418                 /       DOES NOT HAVE ANY INHERENT BOUNDARY RESTRICTIONS.  FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8
 1419                 /       WILL SUPPORT THE "BINCON" UTILITY WITH THE ABILITY TO CREATE EXTENDED-LENGTH
 1420                 /       BINARY FILES THAT CAN BE CONVERTED TO OS/8 BINARY FORMAT AFTER PROCESSING TO
 1421                 /       REMOVE THE PROBLEMS ASSOCIATED WITH THE DEFECTIVE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE DTORG
 1422                 /       DIRECTIVE.  THIS IN TURN WILL FACILITATE FUTURE METHODS OF P?S/8 SYSTEM
 1423                 /       GENERATION.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 32

 1425                 /       SLURP LOADER BLOCK FORMAT.
 1426   
 1427                 /       WITHIN ANY SLURP FORMAT BINARY BLOCK THERE ARE 18 GROUPS OF SEVEN WORDS.  ANY
 1428                 /       ADDITIONAL WORDS ARE IGNORED.
 1429   
 1430                 /       SINCE SLURP LOADERS OPERATE AT THE PHYSICAL DEVICE LEVEL, EACH SLURP LOADER
 1431                 /       HAS THE SPECIFIC RESPONSIBILITY TO ACCESS THE DATA IN A MANNER ANALOGOUS TO A
 1432                 /       TYPICAL DEVICE HANDLER, BUT ALSO HAS THE ADDITIONAL RESPONSIBILITY TO DEAL
 1433                 /       WITH PREVAILING WORDS KNOWN TO EXIST THAT ARE NOT SPECIFICALLY PART OF THE
 1434                 /       BINARY DATA.  SINCE PDP-8 FORMAT DECTAPES ARE 129 WORDS PER BLOCK,
 1435                 /       DECTAPE-SPECIFIC SLURP LOADERS [FOR THE TC01/TC08 AND THE TD8E] MUST IGNORE
 1436                 /       THE THREE ADDITIONAL DATA WORDS IN THE BLOCK [AND ANY ADDITIONAL WORDS THAT
 1437                 /       ARE PRESENT AS PART OF THE OVERALL LOW-LEVEL DECTAPE FORMAT].  [NOTE: SLURP
 1438                 /       LOADERS FOR OTHER DEVICES GENERALLY FUNCTION IN AN ANALOGOUS MANNER; HOWEVER
 1439                 /       CERTAIN DEVICE-SPECIFIC QUIRKS MAY APPLY.]  ALL P?S/8 RELEASES ALSO SUPPORT A
 1440                 /       DEVICE-INDEPENDENT VIRTUAL SLURP LOADER AS A COMMAND-LINE OPTION OF THE "BIN"
 1441                 /       SYSTEM PROGRAM THAT LAUNCHES A SLURP LOADER [WHEN LOADING THE CONTENTS OF
 1442                 /       BINARY FILES, ONE OF SEVERAL OPERATING MODES OF THE BIN PROGRAM]; FOR SYSTEM
 1443                 /       CONFIGURATIONS THAT DO NOT SUPPORT A DEVICE-SPECIFIC SLURP LOADER, THE VIRTUAL
 1444                 /       SLURP LOADER IS ALWAYS USED BY DEFAULT.
 1445   
 1446                 /       NOTE: SLURP BINARY FORMAT IS ALSO USED IN SEVERAL OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS,
 1447                 /       INCLUDING CERTAIN OPERATING SYSTEMS SOLD BY DEC.  [IT IS EXTREMELY LIKELY DEC
 1448                 /       MANAGEMENT PERSONNEL NEVER UNDERSTOOD THIS DESPITE THE OBVIOUS COPYRIGHT
 1449                 /       IMPLICATIONS.]  THESE INCLUDE THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM [WHICH WAS WRITTEN BY
 1450                 /       RICHARD LARY AND LENNY ELEKMAN WITH ADDITIONAL CONTRIBUTIONS BY HANK MAURER,
 1451                 /       JACK BURNESS, JOE FISCHETTI, ROD DORMAN AND CHARLES LASNER], DIBOL-8 [A
 1452                 /       HACKED-UP VARIANT OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM TRIVIALLY CREATED BY HANK MAURER
 1453                 /       TO SUPPORT THE FIRST DIBOL COMPILER AS AN R-L MONITOR SYSTEM PROGRAM] AND ALL
 1454                 /       VARIANT RELEASES OF EDU-SYSTEM 3O [WHICH IS IN TURN BASED ON POLY BASIC, A
 1455                 /       PRODUCT OF RICHARD LARY AND LENNY ELEKMAN WHILE STUDENTS AT BROOKLYN POLYTECH
 1456                 /       AND AS SUCH, NOT EMPLOYEES OF DEC].  POLY BASIC WAS DEVELOPED AS A STANDALONE
 1457                 /       OPERATING SYSTEM ON THE R-L MONITOR, THE PROGENITOR SYSTEM FROM WHICH P?S/8 IS
 1458                 /       DESCENDED; WHILE THERE IS NO INTERNAL FILE SYSTEM ASSOCIATED WITH SLURP FORMAT
 1459                 /       IN THESE BASIC-ONLY SYSTEMS, IT IS THE INTERNAL FORMAT OF ALL BINARY
 1460                 /       OPERATIONS FOR THE INTERNAL RUN-TIME SYSTEM.
 1461   
 1462                 /       SLURP GROUP FORMAT.
 1463   
 1464                 /       EACH OF THE [UP TO] 18 GROUPS IN ANY ONE LOGICAL BLOCK IS PROCESSED IN
 1465                 /       SEQUENCE BEFORE MOVING ON TO THE NEXT BLOCK IN THE FILE [IF APPLICABLE].  THE
 1466                 /       CONTROL [OR FLAG] WORD [FOR THE NEXT SIX DATA WORDS] IS PROCESSED FIRST TO
 1467                 /       DETERMINE THE DISPOSITION OF EACH OF THE SIX DATA WORDS THAT FOLLOW.
 1468   
 1469                 /       EACH CONTROL WORD CONTAINS SIX PAIRS OF CONTROL BITS, EACH FOR THE
 1470                 /       CORRESPONDING DATA WORD.  [BIT PAIRS ARE RECKONED LEFT TO RIGHT.]


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 33

 1472                 /       WITHIN EACH BIT PAIR, THE FOLLOWING DEFINITIONS APPLY:
 1473   
 1474                 /       00 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS DATA TO BE LOADED INTO THE LOCATION INDICATED
 1475                 /            BY THE CURRENT LOADING ORIGIN AND THE CURRENT LOADING FIELD.  THE
 1476                 /            LOADING ORIGIN IS INCREMENTED FOR ANY ADDITIONAL DATA WORD LOADING.
 1477   
 1478                 /       10 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS AN ORIGIN SETTING; THE LOADING ORIGIN WILL BE
 1479                 /            UPDATED TO THE CONTENTS OF THIS WORD FOR ANY ADDITIONAL DATA WORD
 1480                 /            LOADING.
 1481   
 1482                 /       11 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS A FIELD SETTING EXPRESSED AS A "CDF X" [62X1]
 1483                 /            INSTRUCTION TO THE NEW LOADING FIELD X; THE CONTENTS OF THIS WORD WILL BE
 1484                 /            STORED INLINE TO CHANGE THE EFFECTIVE LOADING FIELD FOR ANY ADDITIONAL
 1485                 /            DATA WORDS.
 1486   
 1487                 /       01 - END OF FILE HAS BEEN DETECTED; IGNORE ALL ADDITIONAL DATA WORDS IN THIS
 1488                 /            DATA BLOCK AND ANY ADDITIONAL BLOCKS IN THE CURRENT FILE.  THE SLURP
 1489                 /            LOADER WILL MOVE ON TO PROCESSING ADDITIONAL SLURP FILES [IF APPLICABLE].
 1490   
 1491                 /       NOTE: WHEN APPROPRIATE COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS ARE INVOKED, THE P?S/8 "BIN"
 1492                 /       PROGRAM IS ALSO CAPABLE OF READING BINARY PAPER-TAPES [FROM EITHER THE
 1493                 /       LOW-SPEED OR HIGH-SPEED READER] AND CONVERTING THE BINARY DATA INTO THE
 1494                 /       CONTENTS OF SPECIFIED SLURP BINARY TFS FILES.  USING ADDITIONAL COMMAND-LINE
 1495                 /       OPTIONS, THE SLURP LOADER CAN BE INVOKED IN THE SAME COMMAND TO LOAD THE
 1496                 /       CONTENTS OF THE FILES CREATED EARLIER IN THE COMMAND OPERATION.
 1497   
 1498                 /       NOTE: THE P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM IS ALSO CAPABLE OF ADDITIONAL OPERATIONS TO
 1499                 /       PUNCH THE CONTENTS OF SLURP BINARY FILES OR TO PLACE A COPY OF THE RIM LOADER
 1500                 /       AND A CUSTOMIZED VERSION OF THE BIN LOADER INTO MEMORY WITH A COPY OF THE
 1501                 /       SLURP LOADER BOOTSTRAP AS DEFINED ABOVE.  OTHER THAN THE DEVICE-DEPENDENT
 1502                 /       BOOTSTRAP WORDS, THIS OPERATION IS DEVICE-INDEPENDENT; APPROPRIATE
 1503                 /       COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS ARE AVAILABLE FOR ALL OPERATING MODES OF THE P?S/8 "BIN"
 1504                 /       SYSTEM PROGRAM.
 1505   
 1506                 /       SINCE NO CHECKSUM SCHEME IS USED IN THE SLURP FILE FORMAT, ADVANCED USERS CAN
 1507                 /       FREELY PATCH ANY BINARY FILE AS REQUIRED.  THIS IS USEFUL WHEN MODIFYING
 1508                 /       PAPER-TAPE SOURCED/CONVERTED BINARY FILES WHICH MAY REQUIRE ALTERNATE MEMORY
 1509                 /       FIELDS THAN THE PROGRAM WAS ORIGINALLY ASSEMBLED FOR LOADING.
 1510   
 1511                 /       SELF-STARTING BINARY PROGRAM ISSUES.
 1512   
 1513                 /       "SPARM" [07756] CONTAINS THE 12-BIT STARTING ADDRESS OF THE PROGRAM.  BY
 1514                 /       DIRECTLY LOADING INTO THIS ADDRESS, THE PROGRAM WILL ALWAYS START ACCORDING
 1515                 /       TO THE USER'S PREFERENCE.
 1516   
 1517                 /       "BCIF" [07746] CONTAINS THE STARTING FIELD IN THE FORM OF A "CIF CDF XX"
 1518                 /       INSTRUCTION WHERE XX IS THE STARTING FIELD [IN BITS(6-8)].  [NOTE: THIS
 1519                 /       SPECIFIC ADDRESS APPLIES TO VERSION 8Z AND IS SUBJECT TO CHANGE.]
 1520   
 1521                 /       NOTE: SELF-STARTING INFORMATION IS IGNORED IF THE VIRTUAL BINARY SLURP LOADER
 1522                 /       IS IN EFFECT [AS ALL ATTEMPTS TO LOAD INTO 07600-07777 ARE IGNORED].


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 34

 1524                 /       COMES HERE TO STOP THE LATEST DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT AT THE END OF EVERY FILE.
 1525   
 1526   007605* 1350  BREAD2, TAD     BUNIT                   /GET LATEST UNIT INTO AC[0].
 1527   007606* 6774          SDLC                            /STOP THE LATEST TAPE UNIT WE WERE READING.
 1528   
 1529                 /       FALLS THROUGH TO PROCESS THE NEXT FILE [IF ANY].
 1530   
 1531                 /       THIS IS THE SLURP LOADER STARTING ADDRESS.  THE FILE POINTER MUST BE
 1532                 /       INCREMENTED BEFORE USING TO OBTAIN THE LATEST FILE BLOCK AND UNIT BIT
 1533                 /       DATA.  IF THE LATEST FILE LIST ELEMENT OBTAINED IS 0000 THERE ARE NO
 1534                 /       ADDITIONAL FILES TO LOAD.  [NOTE: IF THE FILE LIST IS THE LARGEST SIZE ENDING
 1535                 /       IN 07777, THE 0000 VALUE IS OBTAINED BECAUSE THE "ISZ BFLPTR" INSTRUCTION
 1536                 /       SKIPS THE NEXT INSTRUCTION.  AS SUCH, ONLY SHORTER FILE LISTS ARE TERMINATED
 1537                 /       WITH A SENTINEL 0000 VALUE PAST THE LAST PASSED FILE ELEMENT.]
 1538   
 1539   007607* 2354  BREAD,  ISZ     BFLPTR                  /BUMP TO NEXT FILE.
 1540   007610* 1754          TAD I   BFLPTR                  /GET THE NEXT FILE HANDLE WORD.
 1541   007611* 0353          AND     B7770/(7770)            /JUST FILE BLOCK BITS.
 1542   007612* 7450          SNA                             /SKIP IF AN ACTVIE ENTRY.
 1543   007613* 5334          JMP     BCIF                    /JUMP IF ALL FILES PROCESSED.
 1544   007614* 3347          DCA     BBLK                    /SAVE LATEST FILE BLOCK.
 1545   007615* 1754          TAD I   BFLPTR                  /GET FILE HANDLE AGAIN.
 1546   007616* 7010          RAR                             /LOW-ORDER UNIT BIT TO LINK.
 1547   007617* 7210          CLA RAR                         /LOW-ORDER UNIT BIT TO AC[0].
 1548   007620* 3350          DCA     BUNIT                   /SAVE UNIT BIT FOR NOW.
 1549   007621* 7710  BCONT,  SPA CLA                         /SKIP NEXT IF CLEAR.
 1550   007622* 7330  BTURNX, NL4000                          /SET REVERSING BIT.
 1551   007623* 1351          TAD     BDIRGO                  /GET CURRENT DIRECTION AND GO BITS.
 1552   007624* 3351          DCA     BDIRGO                  /SAVE [WITH POTENTIALLY UPDATED] DIRECTION.
 1553   007625* 1351          TAD     BDIRGO                  /GET CURRENT DIRECTION AND GO BITS
 1554   007626* 7110          CLL RAR                         /MOVE TO HARDWARE DIRECTION AND GO BITS.
 1555   007627* 1350          TAD     BUNIT                   /ADD ON CURRENT UNIT BIT.
 1556   007630* 6774          SDLC                            /GET THE TAPE MOVING IN THE DESIRED DIRECTION.
 1557   007631* 4327          JMS     BGETWD                  /WAIT FOR
 1558   007632* 4327          JMS     BGETWD                  /SOME LINES.
 1559   007633* 6771  BSRCHMO,SDSS                            /SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
 1560   007634* 5233          JMP     .-1                     /NO, WAIT FOR IT.
 1561   007635* 6776          SDRC                            /YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
 1562   007636* 7106          CLL RTL                         /MOVE UP TWO BITS; DIRECTION TO LINK.
 1563   007637* 0266          AND     B374/(374)              /JUST MARK TRACK BITS.
 1564   007640* 1201          TAD     B7670/(-110)            /COMPARE TO END ZONE VALUE.
 1565   007641* 7450          SNA                             /SKIP IF NOT IN THE END ZONE.
 1566   007642* 5222          JMP     BTURNX                  /REVERSE DIRECTION IF SO.
 1567   007643* 1346          TAD     B7760/(-130+110)        /COMPARE TO BLOCK MARK VALUE.
 1568   007644* 7640          SZA CLA                         /SKIP IF AT BLOCK MARK.
 1569   007645* 5233          JMP     BSRCHMORE               /GO BACK AND KEEP SEARCHING.
 1570   007646* 6777          SDRD                            /GET LATEST BLOCK.
 1571   007647* 7430          SZL                             /SKIP IF GOING FORWARD.
 1572   007650* 1352          TAD     B4002                   /ADD ON UNDERSHOOT VALUE IF REVERSE SEARCH.
 1573   007651* 7041          CIA                             /INVERT FOR TESTING.
 1574   007652* 1347          TAD     BBLK                    /COMPARE TO DESIRED BLOCK.
 1575   007653* 7440          SZA                             /SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
 1576   007654* 5221          JMP     BCONT                   /DETERMINE WHAT TO DO NEXT THERE.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 35

 1578                 /       THE SELECTED DECTAPE IS MOVING FORWARD AND THE DESIRED BLOCK MARK FOUND.
 1579   
 1580                 /       NOW WE NEED TO LOCATE THE 40 MARK WINDOW VALUE [PART OF THE LOCK WORD].
 1581   
 1582   007655* 6771  BFNDLOC,SDSS                            /SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
 1583   007656* 5255          JMP     .-1                     /NO, WAIT FOR IT.
 1584   007657* 6776          SDRC                            /YES, GET THE COMMAND REGISTER.
 1585   007660* 0343          AND     B77/(77)                /JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
 1586   007661* 1265          TAD     B7740/(-40)             /COMPARE TO MARK WINDOW VALUE [LOCK MARK].
 1587   007662* 7640          SZA CLA                         /SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
 1588   007663* 5255          JMP     BFNDLOCK40              /JUMP IF NOT.
 1589   
 1590                 /       READ ONE MORE WORD; WE WILL BE LOCATED AT THE FIRST DATA WORD OF THE BLOCK.
 1591   
 1592   007664* 4327          JMS     BGETWD                  /READ THE LAST NON-DATA WORD.
 1593   007665* 7740  B7740,  SMA SZA CLA                     /CLEAN UP; THIS MIGHT SKIP.
 1594   007666* 0374  B374,   374                             /CONSTANT 0374; EXECUTES AS A HARMLESS
 1595                                                         /"AND" INSTRUCTION IF NOT SKIPPED.
 1596   
 1597                 /       START LOADING THE LATEST BLOCK BINARY DATA.
 1598   
 1599   007667* 1345  BNWREC, TAD     B7756/(-22)             /22 GROUPS IN ONE BLOCK.
 1600   007670* 3340          DCA     BGRPCT                  /INTO GROUP COUNTER.
 1601   007671* 1342  BNWGRP, TAD     B7772/(-6)              /6 BINARY WORDS IN A GROUP.
 1602   007672* 3341          DCA     BWDCT                   /INTO WORD COUNTER.
 1603   007673* 4327          JMS     BGETWD                  /GET LATEST CONTROL WORD.
 1604   007674* 7110          CLL RAR                         /DO AN INITIAL SHIFT.
 1605   007675* 7410          SKP                             /DON'T GET RUNNING VALUE FIRST TIME THROUGH.
 1606   007676* 1337  BGRPLP, TAD     BRLBTS                  /GET CURRENT CONTROL WORD.
 1607   007677* 7006          RTL                             /ROTATE NEXT PAIR TO LINK AND AC[0].
 1608   007700* 3337          DCA     BRLBTS                  /STORE BACK FOR NEXT TIME.
 1609   007701* 1337          TAD     BRLBTS                  /RESTORE LATEST TO AC[0].
 1610   007702* 7710          SPA CLA                         /<EOF> OR FIELD SETTING?
 1611   007703* 5322          JMP     BEOF                    /YES.  [LINK IS OFF IF <EOF>]
 1612   007704* 4327          JMS     BGETWD                  /NO, GET DATA OR ORIGIN VALUE.
 1613   007705* 6201  BCDF,   CDF     00+.-.                  /PREVAILING FIELD BITS WILL BE FILLED IN.
 1614   007706* 7420          SNL                             /SKIP IF ORIGIN SETTING.
 1615   007707* 3736          DCA I   BLOC                    /STORE DATA WORD.
 1616   007710* 6201          CDF     00                      /RESTORE OUR FIELD.
 1617   007711* 2336          ISZ     BLOC                    /BUMP STORAGE POINTER [EVEN IF WRONG TO DO SO].
 1618   007712* 7430          SZL                             /SKIP IF DATA WORD; UNIMPORTANT IF SKIPPED.
 1619   007713* 3336          DCA     BLOC                    /SET NEW ORIGIN IF NOT DATA WORD.
 1620   007714* 2341  BNXTWD, ISZ     BWDCT                   /DONE SIX WORDS IN THIS GROUP YET?
 1621   007715* 5276          JMP     BGRPLP                  /NO, GO DO ANOTHER ONE
 1622   007716* 2340          ISZ     BGRPCT                  /DONE 22 GROUPS IN THIS BLOCK YET?
 1623   007717* 5271          JMP     BNWGRP                  /NO, GO DO ANOTHER ONE.
 1624   
 1625                 /       ALL BINARY DATA IN THIS BLOCK IS NOW LOADED; GO ON TO THE NEXT BLOCK.
 1626   
 1627   007720* 2347          ISZ     BBLK                    /BUMP TO NEXT BLOCK.
 1628   007721* 5221          JMP     BCONT                   /KEEP GOING.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 36

 1630                 /       COMES HERE IF THE LATEST WORD IS EITHER A FIELD SETTING OR AT THE END OF DATA
 1631                 /       WITHIN THE LATEST FILE.
 1632   
 1633   007722* 7620  BEOF,   SNL CLA                         /FIELD SETTING?
 1634   007723* 5205          JMP     BREAD2                  /NO, IT'S THE END OF THIS FILE.
 1635   007724* 4327          JMS     BGETWD                  /YES, GET THE NEXT WORD.
 1636   007725* 3305          DCA     BCDF                    /STORE IN-LINE AS A "CDF" INSTRUCTION
 1637                                                         /TO THE LATEST FIELD.
 1638   007726* 5314          JMP     BNXTWD                  /GO DO ANOTHER WORD.
 1639   
 1640   007727* 0000  BGETWD, .-.                             /GET A NEW WORD ROUTINE.
 1641   007730* 6773          SDSQ                            /QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
 1642   007731* 5330          JMP     .-1                     /NO, WAIT FOR IT.
 1643   007732* 6777          SDRD                            /YES, GET THE DATA WORD.
 1644   007733* 5727          JMP I   BGETWD                  /RETURN TO CALLER.
 1645   
 1646                 /       WHEN ALL FILES HAVE BEEN LOADED, START THE PROGRAM HERE.  THE "BIN" PROGRAM
 1647                 /       STORED THE STARTING FIELD HERE; THE STARTING ADDRESS IS IN "SPARM" [07756].
 1648   
 1649   007734* 6203  BCIF,   .-.^10+CIF CDF                  /CHANGE FIELD TO STARTUP FIELD
 1650   007735* 5756          JMP I   SPARM                   /AND START THE USER PROGRAM.
 1651   
 1652   007736* 0000  BLOC,   .-.                             /MEMORY STORAGE POINTER.
 1653   007737* 0000  BRLBTS, .-.                             /CONTROL FLAGS WORD STORED HERE.
 1654   007740* 0000  BGRPCTR,.-.                             /GROUP COUNTER.
 1655   007741* 0000  BWDCT,  .-.                             /WORD COUNTER.
 1656   
 1657                 /       DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS [SAFE HALT].
 1658   
 1659                 BERR,                                   /DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS.
 1660   007742* 7772  B7772,  SPA SNA SZL CLA HLT             /THIS HALTS.    
 1661   007743* 0077  B77,    77                              /CONSTANT 0077; IF CONTINUE IS PRESSED, THIS
 1662                                                         /EXECUTES AS A HARMLESS "AND" INSTRUCTION THAT
 1663                                                         /CAN BE SKIPPED.
 1664   
 1665                 /       IN CASE CONTINUE IS PRESSED, JMP BACK TO THE SAFE HALT.
 1666   
 1667   007744* 5342          JMP     BERR                    /INFINITE LOOP IF CONTINUE PRESSED.
 1668   
 1669   007745* 7756  B7756,  7756                            /CONSTANT 7756.
 1670   007746* 7760  B7760,  7760                            /CONSTANT 7760.
 1671   007747* 0000  BBLK,   .-.                             /LATEST FILE BLOCK.
 1672   007750* 0000  BUNIT,  .-.                             /CURRENT DRIVE IN BIT[0].
 1673   007751* 2000  BDIRGO, 2000+.-.                        /CURRENT TAPE DIRECTION AND GO BITS.
 1674   007752* 4002  B4002,  4002                            /CONSTANT 4002.
 1675   007753* 7770  B7770,  7770                            /CONSTANT 7770.
 1676   007754* 7756  BFLPTR, SFILES-1                        /FILE LIST POINTER [INCREMENT BEFORE USING].
 1677   007755* 0000          0
 1678   007756* 7742  SPARM,  BERR                            /STARTING ADDRESS PASSED HERE [DEFAULT SET].
 1679   007757* 0000  SFILES, ZBLOCK  -.                      /FILE HANDLES PASSED HERE.
 1680   
 1681          *4600          RELOC                           /TURN OFF RELOCATION.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 37

 1683                 /       P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOADER.
 1684   
 1685                 /       WHEN THE DEVICE-DEPENDENT BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER [AS OPPOSED TO THE
 1686                 /       DEVICE-INDEPENDENT VIRTUAL BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER SUPPORTED ON ALL P?S/8
 1687                 /       SYSTEMS] IS IN EFFECT, THE CONTENTS OF 07600-07777 NO LONGER CONTAIN THE
 1688                 /       SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; A REBOOT ROUTINE IS PROVIDED AT 07600 [AND SOME
 1689                 /       ADDITIONAL WORDS FOLLOWING] TO ALLOW A TOTAL SYSTEM RELOAD CONSISTENT WITH
 1690                 /       THE MORE USUAL RESTARTING OF THE SYSTEM AT THE SAME ADDRESS.
 1691   
 1692                 /       AT FIRST GLANCE, IT WOULD APPEAR THAT AS PART OF THE TRADEOFF OF LOADER
 1693                 /       CHOICE, EITHER MORE EFFICIENT BINARY FILE LOADING [WITH NO NEED TO
 1694                 /       WRITE-ENABLE THE SYSTEM DEVICE] AND THE LACK OF ACCESS TO THE NORMAL SYSTEM
 1695                 /       KERNEL IS PROVIDED [OR THE SYSTEM DEVICE MUST BE WRITE-ENABLED AND THE SYSTEM
 1696                 /       KERNEL IS RESIDENT IN MEMORY AS IS NORMALLY THE CASE].
 1697   
 1698                 /       HOWEVER, THIS IS NOT THE CASE.  BY INVOKING THE P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM FOR BINARY
 1699                 /       FILE LOADING AND INCLUDING THE '/I' COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCH, AT THE END OF
 1700                 /       ALL LOADING OF THE ENTIRETY OF THE CONTENTS OF ALL BINARY FILES, IT IS
 1701                 /       POSSIBLE TO ALSO HAVE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AS WELL!  THERE ARE A FEW
 1702                 /       PROVISOS:
 1703   
 1704                 /       1)      THE BLOCK JUST PAST THE END OF THE LAST FILE LOADED FROM THE SYSTEM
 1705                 /               DEVICE WILL DETERMINE THE FINAL POSITION OF THE DEVICE.  [NOTE:
 1706                 /               THE DESCRIPTION USED HERE ENVISIONS DECTAPE AND RELATED SYSTEMS].
 1707                 /               HOWEVER, IF THE '/I' COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCH IS INVOKED, THERE WILL BE
 1708                 /               SEVERAL ADDITIONAL DEVICE OPERATIONS THAT WILL TEND TO LEAVE THE
 1709                 /               SYSTEM DEVICE POSITIONED JUST PAST BLOCK 0000 [WHICH WILL BE RELOADED
 1710                 /               AS PART OF THE EXTRA TAPE MOTION].
 1711   
 1712                 /               ASSUMING A PROGRAM NORMALLY [EVENTUALLY] EXITS TO 07600, THIS IS NOT
 1713                 /               AN OVERALL ISSUE PER SE; THE DEVICE MUST BE REWOUND TO BLOCK 0000 AS
 1714                 /               PART OF THE SYSTEM RESTART OPERATION.  AS SUCH, THIS IS MERELY A
 1715                 /               MATTER OF REWINDING BEFORE THE PROGRAM STARTS AS OPPOSED TO WHEN THE
 1716                 /               PROGRAM EXITS.
 1717   
 1718                 /       2)      PART OF THE ADDITIONAL OPERATIONS IS A BINARY LOADING OPERATION OF
 1719                 /               BLOCK 0060 ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  THIS IS THE MECHANISM THAT PERFORMS
 1720                 /               THE SEEMINGLY IMPOSSIBLE TASK OF CONVERTING THE CONTENTS OF MEMORY
 1721                 /               LOCATIONS 07600-07777 FROM THE SYSTEM RESTART BOOTSTAP [AND BINARY
 1722                 /               LOADER ARTIFACTS] INTO THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 [THE IMAGE OF THE
 1723                 /               SYSTEM KERNEL].
 1724   
 1725                 /       3)      BLOCK 0060 IS APPENDED TO THE LIST OF SLURP BINARY INPUT FILES; THE
 1726                 /               RELOAD OPERATION MUST BE PERFORMED AFTER THE NORMAL BINARY LOADING IS
 1727                 /               OTHERWISE COMPLETE.  AS SUCH, BLOCK 0060 BECOMES THE LAST FILE HANDLE
 1728                 /               IN THE PASSED FILE LIST.  [THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0060 ARE GENERALLY
 1729                 /               CONSISTENT WITH A SMALL SLURP BINARY FILE THAT ENDS WITHIN THE FIRST
 1730                 /               BLOCK.]
 1731   
 1732                 /               IF THE '/I' COMMAND-LINE OPTION IS IN EFFECT, THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF
 1733                 /               INPUT FILES IS LOWERED FROM 17 TO 16 TO ENSURE A FILE HANDLE CAN BE
 1734                 /               PLACED AT THE END OF THE FILE LIST.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 38

 1736                 /               SINCE SLURP BINARY FILE FORMAT APPROACHES 6/7 OF THE LOADING
 1737                 /               EFFICIENCY OF CORE-IMAGE DATA, THIS LOWERS THE MAXIMUM AMOUNT OF
 1738                 /               BINARY DATA THAT CAN BE LOADED WITH A SINGLE COMMAND LINE FROM
 1739                 /               APPROXIMATELY 7.25 MEMORY FIELDS TO 6.9 MEMORY FIELDS; HOWEVER, SINCE
 1740                 /               TYPICAL PROGRAM LOADING FOR PRACTICAL PROGRAMMING PROJECTS SELDOM
 1741                 /               EXCEEDS 8K OF PROGRAM MEMORY, THIS IS NOT AN IMPORTANT CONSIDERATION.
 1742                 /               [NOTE: EXTREMELY LARGE PROGRAMS MIGHT REQUIRE TWO COMMANDS TO LOAD
 1743                 /               ALL PROGRAM COMPONENTS.]
 1744                 /               
 1745                 /               FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 WILL INCLUDE A BINARY CONVERSION PROGRAM
 1746                 /               KNOWN AS "BINCON" WHICH HAS SEVERAL MODES OF OPERATION.  ADVANCED
 1747                 /               USERS OF BINCON WILL BE ABLE TO CONCATENATE THE CONTENTS OF A GROUP OF
 1748                 /               TFS SLURP BINARY FILES INTO A SINGLE EXTENDED-LENGTH FILE; AS SUCH,
 1749                 /               PROGRAM LOADING WILL BE MORE EASILY ACCOMPLISHED IN FEWER UNIQUE FILE
 1750                 /               NAMES.  THE MINOR RESTRICTION ON MAXIMUM PASSED FILES WILL BECOME
 1751                 /               UNIMPORTANT.
 1752   
 1753                 /       THE FOLLOWING PROGRAMMING GENERATES THE IMAGE IN MEMORY OF A CONTRIVED SLURP
 1754                 /       BINARY FILE TO BE WRITTEN OUT TO BLOCK 0060 OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  [NOTE:
 1755                 /       SINCE THIS BLOCK IS READ ONLY BY THE SLURP LOADER, IT DOES NOT HAVE TO CONFORM
 1756                 /       TO THE USUAL ORIGIN AND FIELD SETTING CONVENTIONS; THIS IS DONE TO MINIMIZE
 1757                 /       THE SIZE OF THE DATA, ALL OF WHICH MUST FIT INTO A SINGLE BLOCK.]
 1758   
 1759           0002          FIELD   IGEN&70%10              /RELOADER BLOCK GENERATION FIELD.
 1760   
 1761          *4000          *IGEN&7600                      /RELOADER BLOCK GENERATION ADDRESS.
 1762   
 1763                 ICOR,   NOPUNCH                         /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1764   
 1765           0000          FIELD   0                       /WHERE THIS LOADS.
 1766   
 1767          *4000          *ICOR                           /RESTORE ORIGIN [IN CASE FIELD CHANGES IT].
 1768   
 1769                         ENPUNCH                         /UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1770   
 1771   004000  5640          OR^4+FL^4+OR^4+OR^4+DA^4+DA     /CONTROL WORD.
 1772   
 1773   004001  0200          0200                            /*200.  NOTE: THIS IS SUPERFLUOUS.
 1774   
 1775   004002  6201          CDF     00                      /FIELD 0.
 1776   
 1777   004003  0200          0200                            /*200.  NOTE: THIS IS SUPERFLUOUS.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 39

 1779                 /       MOST OF THE CODE LOADS IN OVER THE [NOW UNNEEDED] FILE LIST AREA.
 1780   
 1781   004004  7757          SFILES                          /*SFILES; THIS IS WHERE THE CODE STARTS LOADING.
 1782   
 1783          *7757          RELOC   SFILES                  /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1784   
 1785                 /       SINCE THE RELOADER SLURP FILE BLOCK IS THE LAST ELEMENT IN THE FILE LIST, THE
 1786                 /       ENTIRE FILE LIST AREA IS AVAILABLE FOR THE PURPOSE OF [SELECTIVELY] USING THE
 1787                 /       SLURP LOADER CODE FOR THE PURPOSE OF RELOADING THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  THE
 1788                 /       FOLLOWING CODE LOADS IN THE FILE LIST AREA WHICH IS ALLOWED AT THIS POINT.
 1789   
 1790   007757* 7600  RLPOINT,SBOOT/CLA!400                   /CLEAR THE AC; ALSO STORAGE POINTER.
 1791   
 1792   007760* 1334          TAD     BCIF                    /GET PROGRAM STARTING FIELD INSTRUCTION.
 1793   
 1794   007761* 0000          DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA     /NEXT CONTROL WORD.
 1795   
 1796          *7761          RELOC   .-1                     /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1797   
 1798   007761* 3372          DCA     7772                    /STORE OUT OF HARM'S WAY.
 1799   
 1800                 /       NOW READ THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IN ONE WORD AT A TIME.
 1801   
 1802   007762* 6773  RELOOP, SDSQ                            /QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
 1803   007763* 5362          JMP     .-1                     /NO, WAIT FOR IT.
 1804   007764* 6777          SDRD                            /YES, GET THE DATA WORD.
 1805   007765* 3757          DCA I   RLPOINTER               /STORE A WORD.
 1806   007766* 2357          ISZ     RLPOINTER               /BUMP TO NEXT.
 1807   
 1808   007767* 0000          DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA     /NEXT CONTROL WORD.
 1809   
 1810          *7767          RELOC   .-1                     /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1811   
 1812   007767* 2374          ISZ     RLCNTR                  /DONE ENOUGH WORDS?
 1813   007770* 5362          JMP     RELOOP                  /NO, KEEP GOING.
 1814   007771* 6774          SDLC                            /YES, STOP THE DECTAPE.
 1815   007772* 6203  RLCIF,  .-.^10+CIF CDF                  /WILL BE THE STARTING FIELD INSTRUCTION.
 1816   007773* 5756          JMP I   SPARM                   /GO START THE PROGRAM UP.
 1817   
 1818   007774* 7622  RLCNTR, SBOOT-SPARM                     /COUNT OF WORDS TO MOVE.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 40

 1820                 /       THE RELOADER CODE HAS NOW BEEN READ INTO THE FILE LIST AREA; THE SLURP LOADER
 1821                 /       WILL NOW BE MODIFIED TO READ IN THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000.
 1822   
 1823   007775* 4210          OR^4+DA^4+OR^4+DA^4+OR^4+DA     /NEXT CONTROL WORD.
 1824   
 1825                 /       ALL OF THE RELOADER CODE WORDS HAVE BEEN READ IN.  THE SLURP LOADER IS NO
 1826                 /       LONGER NEEDED FOR ITS NORMAL PURPOSE.  THE SLURP LOADER WILL NOW BE MODIFIED
 1827                 /       TO CAUSE IT TO READ IN BLOCK 0000 DATA.
 1828   
 1829                 /       AFTER THE END OF FILE IS DETECTED IN THE [STILL-FUNCTIONING] SLURP LOADER, THE
 1830                 /       CODE GOES BACK TO [NORMALLY] DETECT THE END OF THE FILE LIST [WHICH WOULD THEN
 1831                 /       START THE PROGRAM UP].  BEFORE THAT HAPPENS, IT WILL BE MODIFIED TO INSTEAD
 1832                 /       SEARCH FOR BLOCK 0000.  WHEN THE SEARCH COMPLETES AND BLOCK 0000 DATA IS
 1833                 /       AVAILABLE, PREVENT THE SLURP LOADER READ OPERATIONS, AND INSTEAD EXECUTE THE
 1834                 /       RELOAD CODE READ IN OVER THE [AVAILABLE] FILE LIST AREA.
 1835   
 1836          *7667          RELOC   BNWREC                  /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1837   
 1838   007667* 7667          BNWREC                          /*BNWREC.
 1839   
 1840   007670* 5357          JMP     RLPOINTER               /RELOAD BLOCK 0000 INSTEAD OF SLURP LOADING.
 1841   
 1842                 /       THE SLURP LOADER SEARCH ROUTINE SUPPORTS BOTH DRIVE UNITS.  THE FOLLOWING CODE
 1843                 /       FORCES ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM DRIVE UNIT [0].
 1844   
 1845          *7627          RELOC   BTURNX+5                /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
 1846   
 1847   007627* 7627          BTURNX+5                        /*BTURNX+5.
 1848   
 1849   007630* 7000          NOP                             /THIS FORCES THE SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT [0].
 1850   
 1851                 /       WHEN THE SLURP LOADER GOES BACK TO DETECT ANOTHER FILE, FORCE IT TO INSTEAD
 1852                 /       USE BLOCK 0000 AND START A SEARCH ON THAT BLOCK.
 1853   
 1854          *7607          RELOC   BREAD                   /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER
 1855   
 1856   007607* 7607          BREAD                           /*BREAD.
 1857   
 1858   007610* 5214          JMP     BREAD+5                 /FORCE BLOCK 0000 TO BE LOCATED FOR READING.
 1859   
 1860          *7771          RELOC   SFILES+12               /FOOL THE ASSEMBLER [REALLY DOESN'T MATTER].
 1861   
 1862   007771* 2525          EF^4+EF^4+EF^4+EF^4+EF^4+EF     /NEXT CONTROL WORD.
 1863   
 1864   007772* 0000          ZBLOCK  6                       /NO DATA, JUST END OF FILE.
 1865   
 1866                 /       THIS IS THE END OF FILE.  THE SLURP LOADER WILL NOW GO BACK AND START LOADING
 1867                 /       IN [MOST OF] BLOCK 0000.


/     P?S/8 SYSTEM HANDLER TD8E/MR8E-C        P?S PAL V08S    SAT  05-MAR-16  PAGE 41

 1869          *4043          RELOC                           /TURN OFF RELOCATION.
 1870   
 1871                 /       END OF THE RELOADER FILE IMAGE WHICH MUST FIT IN ONE PAGE.  CHECK TO MAKE SURE
 1872                 /       THE CODE DOES NOT EXCEED THIS RESTRICTION.  [MOST SLURP FILES ARE SEVERAL
 1873                 /       BLOCKS LONG; THIS IS NOT CONSIDERED A "FILE".]
 1874   
